WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:07.570
Film Courage: Do you start every screenplay out with
an outline? David Wappel, Screenwriter: I first start with a notebook

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:12.820
once I decided okay this is a project
that I want to write I buy a notebook

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.470
for it I usually try to sort of theme
the notebook so if it's like old like

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.650
pirate adventure I'll try to make it
look I'll find a notebook that looks

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:27.579
like that and then I will just use that
notebook to just freehand write anything

00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:31.479
I'm interested in in regards to this
screenplay whether it's just like a very

00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:38.830
vague idea of I think this thing should
happen or I'm writing dialogue for a

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.910
scene that might exist in the stream I'm
sort of filling that notebook up and I

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.900
don't really have a timeline at that
point so I'm talking about specs scripts

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.780
for me I'm just writing it writing it
write it until hopefully a story will

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:59.170
coalesce and once I feel like that
story's coalesced I'll try to write that

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.130
story as succinctly impossible
essentially I'm trying to write the log

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:11.229
line for it and if that log line is
engaging to me and I think will move me

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:16.950
if I think that story that is told even
in that little you know one sentence is

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.369
moving enough to me I'll try to take
that sentence and I'll turn it into a

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:26.439
paragraph and if that paragraph is
moving then I'll move on if it's not I

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:31.270
will continue to maybe reshape that
paragraph and then that paragraph will

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:36.490
become a page that page will become
multiple pages into sort of a treatment

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.319
and then if the treatment that's when I
think I'm doing most of my story work

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:47.700
I'm writing that over and over and I
will do that for a couple a months maybe

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:54.340
depending on how often I can work on it
and once that treatment is really

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.889
resonating with me and is a story that
again will move me then I can take it

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:03.130
and start writing the draft and so it
almost feels like that draft is more

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:09.700
like a translation project than writing
something new I'm taking something that

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:16.480
exists in full prose writing
and then put it into a screenplay format

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:25.450
and so all of the little things that
exist in a screenplay that's when I'm

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.660
putting those things in because my story
I feel I already feel incredibly

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.810
comfortable about so I don't have to
discover any of the story things they

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.200
just discover all those little things
all those little nooks and crannies that

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.010
happen when a story is now gonna be put
on the screen and I'm translating it

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.690
there and then I'll get that first draft
and then from that I'm moving into

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:48.489
rewrites and polishes and all those sort
of conversations but I think it's helped

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:53.920
me because I feel like my story usually
doesn't go through too many significant

00:02:53.920 --> 00:03:01.780
changes once I'm on that draft once I'm
in the screenplay software and writing

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.220
it because all those changes I've hit
all those dead ends all those things

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.220
that I thought were great that are not
all those things that I was excited

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.630
about that didn't end up doing anything
for me I've already figured that out in

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:18.940
that treatment process so I don't
outline so much as try to just tell a

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:26.890
story and tell it fuller and fuller
until that's a full rich story and then

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.530
translate it to a screenplay if that
makes sense

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:33.069
it does so it almost sounds like this
little notebook that you buy this themed

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:37.450
it's like a scrapbook yeah okay so then
you're putting it's almost like a diet

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.109
part diary parts scrapbook you're just
putting little notes and it's like it's

00:03:41.109 --> 00:03:46.720
like filling up and then by the time you
get to the screenplay formatting it's

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.209
already its own world in that little
book yeah it already exists and I have

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:57.160
this full treatment that was pretty
detailed and it tells a story so it's

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.670
not an outline because I don't so I
don't have to discover how this

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.540
character is going to convince this
other character of what they want

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.889
because I've done that in the treatment
but now I get to in the screenplay

00:04:08.889 --> 00:04:13.829
figure out what are the exact words that
she's using to convince this character

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:20.320
about what she wants or something like
that so a lot of the work is for me

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.430
compared to how I think some other
people I've heard their process some of

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.370
that work is kind of split where it's
story stuff here and

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:33.910
then sort of screenplay stuff over here
when I'm in the software and and doing

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:35.830
all the screenplay formatting and stuff
like that

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:44.770
are there any pictures to help flush it
out no I actually don't use pictures I

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:53.889
think it's because I'm a horrible artist
and I don't do clippings or anything

00:04:53.889 --> 00:05:01.419
that I think in every way I've ever been
creative it's always been in words and

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:07.840
ideas and so anytime I've ever tried to
translate any of my creative ideas

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:16.539
through visual stuff it just never works
and so I'm just I've just I think given

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:21.789
up on that for now maybe at some point I
will try to learn how to draw or

00:05:21.789 --> 00:05:25.810
something like that but it's probably
why I do I'm a lot more active on

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.810
Twitter than I am on Instagram because
that's just where I feel the most

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:31.539
comfortable
sure well Instagram is also very

